Welcome to OOFEM Element Library manual README.

What is here
------------
The element library manual: the supported element types, their degrees of
freedom, the numbering of their edges and surfaces, and the parameters of each.
One page per element group under ``pages/``.

Prerequisities
----------------
To build the documentation, you need Sphinx (Python documentation generator,
https://www.sphinx-doc.org).
Typically invoke ``pip install -U Sphinx`` to install it on your system.

Building the PDF additionally needs a LaTeX installation; see doc/README for
the list of packages.

The element figures are drawn with TikZ.  They are rendered properly when
``sphinxcontrib-tikz`` is installed (``pip install -U sphinxcontrib-tikz``);
without it the build still succeeds and shows the TikZ source as a captioned
literal block, which is what ``doc/_ext/oofemtikz.py`` falls back to.

Building documentation
----------------------
1) Change working directory to doc/elementlibmanual inside your oofem source
   tree
2) run ``make html`` to build the html version, in ``_build/html``
3) for the pdf version run ``make latex``, then ``make all-pdf`` in
   ``_build/latex``

If a change does not seem to take effect, run ``make clean`` first: the build
reuses the parsed documents cached in ``_build/doctrees``.

Math macros
-----------
The equations use ``\mbf``, ``\del``, ``\der`` and ``\pard``, inherited from
``elementlibmanual.tex``.  All four are common to the manuals and come from
``doc/_ext/oofemmath.py``, which defines them for MathJax (html) and in the
LaTeX preamble (pdf) alike, so nothing has to be declared here.  Anything
specific to this manual goes in an ``oofem_math_macros`` dict in ``conf.py`` --
never in ``mathjax3_config`` alone, which renders in the browser but breaks the
pdf build.

Writing equations
-----------------
A ``.. math::`` block is wrapped by Sphinx in a ``split`` environment, which
allows a single alignment ``&`` per row.  Write ``a &= b``, not the ``eqnarray``
form ``a &=& b``: MathJax tolerates the second, LaTeX does not.

Figures
-------
pdflatex cannot include SVG.  Where a figure is an SVG, put a PDF of it next to
the SVG in ``figures/`` and reference it without an extension::

    .. figure:: /figures/lattice3d.*

Sphinx then uses the SVG for html and the PDF for the pdf build.

Tables
------
Sphinx only honours the column widths drawn in a grid table when the table
carries an explicit ``:widths:`` option; without it every column gets an equal
share, which gives the label column far too much room.  Keep the ``:widths:``
line on the table directive, matching the geometry drawn below it.

A cell holding a lone ``-`` is read as an empty bullet list, not a dash.  Write
``\-``.

Shared configuration
--------------------
The page width, figure sizing and record styling come from
``doc/_static/oofem.css``, and the author list on the title page from
``doc/_ext/oofemlatex.py``.  Do not set page_width or body_max_width in
html_theme_options; see section [4] of doc/README.

Happy contributing!
